- `packages/material-ui-lab/src/TreeItem/TreeItem.test.js`

In `packages/material-ui-lab/src/TreeItem/TreeItem.js`, it changes the `React.useEffect` inside the `TreeItem` component that registers a node’s children with `addNodeToNodeMap`.

Previously, it assumed every child was a valid React element:

```js
const childIds = React.Children.map(children, child => child.props.nodeId) || [];
```

The change instead:

- Initializes an empty `childIds` array.
- Iterates with `React.Children.forEach`.
- Uses `React.isValidElement(child)` before reading `child.props`.
- Adds the ID only when the child has a `nodeId`.
- Performs this work only when `addNodeToNodeMap` exists.

Conceptually:

```js
if (addNodeToNodeMap) {
  const childIds = [];

  React.Children.forEach(children, child => {
    if (React.isValidElement(child) && child.props.nodeId) {
      childIds.push(child.props.nodeId);
    }
  });

  addNodeToNodeMap(nodeId, childIds);
}
```

This makes `null`, `false`, and other non-element children produced by conditional rendering safe, while ensuring hidden children are removed from the parent’s registered child-ID list.

In `packages/material-ui-lab/src/TreeItem/TreeItem.test.js`, it adds the test case `should allow conditional child`. The test defines a stateful `TestComponent` containing:

- An expanded parent `TreeItem`.
- A child rendered through `!hide && <TreeItem ... />`.
- A button that changes `hide` to `true`.

It verifies that after clicking the button:

- The child disappears.
- The parent remains.
- The parent no longer has `aria-expanded`, confirming that it is treated as having no children.

